---
/**
 * foodcraft / TestimonialsBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar" REVIEWS: a CENTRED head (the
 * lilac eyebrow pill over a Baloo 2 headline and one small muted line) above a
 * FULL-BLEED HORIZONTAL RAIL of soft white quote cards that snap as you swipe —
 * each card opened by a rating mark, then the quote in Nunito Sans at 16.5px,
 * and an attribution pinned to the card floor beside a round initial badge.
 *
 * ROLE: the social proof a stranger reads before deciding to walk in — which is
 * exactly why this file prints nothing the owner did not put on record.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * `data-component="TESTIMONIALS"` element of "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html":
 *   section       padding 80px 0 0, overflow:hidden — the shared wrapper
 *                 `section` rhythm plus the clip that lets the rail bleed.
 *   head          max-width 1200 / 28px gutter — drawn with the shared .wrap
 *                 primitive, which centres min(--maxw, 92vw) — centred,
 *                 margin-bottom 30px
 *   eyebrow       11px / .14em / caps / 700, #5C3580 ON a #EEE2F5 pill,
 *                 padding 8px 16px, radius 100px
 *   headline      Baloo 2 700, clamp(30px,4vw,52px)/1.08, margin .24em 0 6px
 *   note          13px / 600 / #6B6067, margin 0
 *   rail          display:flex, gap 16px, overflow-x:auto, padding 0 28px 20px,
 *                 scroll-snap-type: x mandatory
 *   card          <figure>, margin 0, flex:none, width 360px,
 *                 scroll-snap-align:center, #FFFFFF, radius 26px,
 *                 padding 28px 26px, flex column, gap 14px
 *   mark          14px, letter-spacing 2px
 *   quote         16.5px / 1.72, #241A22, margin 0 (BODY face, not the display
 *                 face — the design sets no font-family on the blockquote, so it
 *                 inherits Nunito Sans; declared here so nothing can leak in)
 *   attribution   margin-top auto, flex row, align centre, gap 11px
 *   badge         38x38 circle, Baloo 2 16px/700, one of the design's tinted
 *                 pairs
 *   name          13px / 600 / #6B6067
 * Mapped to the wrapper tokens holding those exact values: #FFF7F2 = --paper,
 * #FFFFFF = --card, #241A22 = --ink, #6B6067 = --muted, #7B4FA8 = --accent,
 * #5C3580 = --accent-deep, #EEE2F5 = --lilac, #F2C6DA = --pink,
 * #F7DCE7 = --pink-2, #5C2A45 = --plum, #E3EFDC = --sage, #3C5C2A = --on-sage,
 * 26px = --r-lg, 100px = --r-pill, Baloo 2 = --disp, Nunito Sans = --sans,
 * 1200px = --maxw. NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS BELOW.
 *
 * THE ONE COLOUR IN THIS ELEMENT WITH NO TOKEN, stated rather than invented:
 * the design's star row is #9A6510 and its fourth badge pair is #6B4A18 on
 * #FBEBD6 — a warm amber the wrapper never names (it declares purple, pink,
 * sage, lilac and plum, and no amber at all; --tint and --well are both cool).
 * Rather than write a hex, the stars take --accent, the brand purple that leads
 * this palette, and the badge cycles the THREE tokenised pairs only. Flagged in
 * the handover report as the one deliberate divergence from the design file.
 *
 * THE THREE HUES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, so: --pink appears here ONCE and only
 * as a GROUND — the small rounded bar that replaces a star row on a review with
 * no rating. Nothing in this file paints text with --pink or --sage, and no
 * purple is ever set on a pink ground (that pair is 3.93 and is how this design
 * goes wrong). The card sits on --card, not on a band, so this file declares no
 * .band-pink / .band-sage / .band-lilac: the design gives this section the plain
 * blush page ground.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX `sip-`, chosen for THIS template. It is deliberately NOT the
 * `tah-` set from TestimonialsBM nor the `rg-` set before it: an audit had to
 * rename a whole class set that had been copied verbatim from a sibling, and two
 * templates sharing a scoped class name makes every future diff read as a shared
 * component that is not one. Only the house STYLE was copied from BM — this doc
 * block's shape, the coercion helpers, the guard discipline.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME NOTE (theme engine): the section must NOT be class "testi" —
 * buildOverrideCss pins `.testi, .testi p { color: … !important }` and would
 * repaint every quote with a scheme colour this design never chose. It is
 * "sip-reviews", and it emits no button at all, so no engine label pin
 * (.btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .nav-cta / .cta-band / .trust) reaches anything
 * here. Buttons in this template are .btn--fill / .btn--pink / .btn--cream /
 * .btn--line; this section draws none because the design draws none.
 *
 * READS content.testimonials.items ({quote, who, rating}); tolerates the legacy
 * bare array parked at content.testimonials and the legacy author/name
 * spellings for the attribution. The data-field always names the CANONICAL
 * path, so the first sidebar edit migrates a legacy row onto the real key.
 *
 * COERCED AT EVERY READ — THE ROW FIRST, THEN EVERY FIELD. Nothing upstream
 * type-checks either one. astro-builder's normalizeBlock passes a non-object
 * entry straight through, so `items` can hold a null, and reading `.quote` off
 * that null throws at BUILD time and takes the whole page down. So each row is
 * normalised to an object before a single field is read, and a bare STRING row
 * is read as the quote itself — the legacy shape where the row IS the quote —
 * so owner text is never silently dropped. Then the fields: a quote arriving as
 * a number crashes the build on .trim(); one arriving as an object prints
 * "[object Object]" through set:html. str() passes strings and finite numbers
 * and treats everything else as absent. Blank-after-trim counts as absent too —
 * a whitespace-only `who` must not draw an empty attribution row holding the
 * card's bottom gap open, and a whitespace-only tag must not draw an empty
 * lilac pill (a pill with no word in it is a floating blob).
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "testimonials" — a data-field on a path the schema does not own is a
 * dead hook that no sidebar input can focus)
 *   testimonials.tag                  — the lilac eyebrow pill
 *   testimonials.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place this
 *                                       design's signature emphasis, the PURPLE
 *                                       COLOUR CHANGE, anywhere inside their own
 *                                       sentence. It is a colour change and not
 *                                       an italic because Baloo 2 ships no
 *                                       italic and asking for one synthesises an
 *                                       oblique that fights its rounded
 *                                       terminals; the wrapper already styles
 *                                       .h2 em / .h2 .em / .em that way, which
 *                                       is why no scoped rule restates it.
 *   testimonials.source               — the small muted line under the headline.
 *                                       The design's own demo text there is a
 *                                       swipe hint; a hint is UI copy and this
 *                                       file writes no copy, so the slot is
 *                                       bound to the one declared field that
 *                                       lives in that position ("Source line")
 *                                       and the line simply does not exist until
 *                                       the owner types into it.
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.quote     — set:html, the blockquote
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.who       — the attribution beside the badge
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.rating    — read, never printed as text: it is the
 *                                       ONLY thing that can draw a star (below).
 *                                       It deliberately carries NO data-field on
 *                                       the star row, and that is not a missing
 *                                       hook — the row RENDERS the number, it is
 *                                       not the number. editorBridge's ed:update
 *                                       would replace the glyphs with the raw
 *                                       digit, and VisualEditor (which does not
 *                                       honour data-derived) makes every
 *                                       [data-field] contentEditable, so an
 *                                       admin could type stars back into rating
 *                                       and lose the score. It is edited in the
 *                                       sidebar's "Rating out of 5" box, which
 *                                       genericContentSchema declares.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored: the number of stars; the badge INITIAL (the first
 * character of the attribution the owner typed, uppercased in CSS rather than in
 * the string so nothing is mangled — toUpperCase turns a German sharp s into two
 * letters); which of the three tinted badge pairs a card gets (display position,
 * so it stays an even cycle after a blank row is dropped); and the reveal delay,
 * capped at 3 because the wrapper only defines delays 1-3 and a fourth card must
 * not wait forever.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION — the hard rule of this section:
 *   - THE STAR ROW IS NOT HARDCODED. The design prints five stars over every
 *     quote, because the designer was filling in their own template. Five stars
 *     for a customer who never gave a score is a claim invented on the owner's
 *     behalf, so stars are drawn ONLY from a finite numeric
 *     testimonials.items.${i}.rating. Non-numeric, blank and absent ratings draw
 *     no stars at all; the card instead opens with a short rounded --pink bar
 *     that occupies the same beat and states nothing — a soft mark, which is
 *     what a design with no sharp corner anywhere wants. Expect that bar on
 *     essentially every real site: it is the correct, honest default, not a
 *     missing feature.
 *   - CLAMPING, stated so it is not read as an oversight: a rating is rounded,
 *     then capped at 5 (a typo of "7" draws five stars, never seven). A value
 *     that rounds BELOW 1 — 0, -2, 0.4 — is treated as no usable rating and
 *     draws the bar. It is never clamped UP to one star, because inventing a
 *     star out of a zero is the same fabrication as inventing five.
 *   - The accessible name announces the SAME number that is drawn, so the label
 *     and the glyphs can never disagree.
 *   - NO review count, NO average score, NO platform name, NO "students' choice"
 *     badge. None of it is in the data contract, and the design draws none.
 *   - Quote and attribution are guarded independently and every guard sits
 *     OUTSIDE its tag, so an unattributed quote renders as a quote rather than
 *     as an empty row with a badge showing no letter.
 *   - A row with no quote is DROPPED: the card IS the quote, so a name-only row
 *     would draw a white card attributing nothing to somebody. The row stays in
 *     the sidebar and reappears the instant a quote is typed.
 *
 * SECTION GATE: the whole <section> renders only when at least one row has a
 * real quote. It deliberately does NOT count tag / headline / source. Those
 * three arrive from the admin sidebar or the AI extractor with nothing behind
 * them all the time, and lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills heads across the
 * site, so a gate counting a headline would ship a centred heading over an empty
 * rail — the one thing a proof section must never do.
 *
 * ORIGINAL INDEXES: `i` is captured BEFORE blank rows are dropped, so deleting
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 4. The two DECORATIVE things
 * that do vary per card — the badge tint and the reveal delay — use the display
 * position `k` instead, because a gap in the cycle after a dropped row would be
 * visible.
 *
 * THE ONE AUTHORED STRING in this file is the stars' aria-label, which is never
 * visible. It exists because a row of glyphs with no accessible name is
 * decoration announcing nothing, and a rating the owner typed is information a
 * screen-reader user is entitled to. No visible word on this page comes from
 * this file.
 *
 * KEYBOARD ACCESS TO THE RAIL. A horizontally scrolling region whose cards hold
 * no focusable element is unreachable by keyboard in every browser that has not
 * shipped auto-focusable scrollers, so the rail takes tabindex="0" and picks up
 * the wrapper's shared focus ring through its :where([tabindex]) selector. Its
 * accessible name is the OWNER'S OWN HEADLINE via aria-labelledby — never a
 * literal written here — so when there is no headline the rail stays focusable
 * and scrollable but is announced with no name, rather than with a name this
 * file invented. The role/label pair is spread from a single object so a missing
 * headline can never emit a duplicated or empty attribute.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   testimonials.bigQuote — there is no pull-quote element in this design.
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.role — the row schema stops at quote/who/rating, so
 *                           the hook would focus nothing in the v3 sidebar.
 *   No image: the badge is a derived letter on a tinted circle, not a photo, so
 *   there is no .plate, no .ph, no .cap, no role="img" on an upload and nothing
 *   that would need an owner-authored accessible name for a picture.
 *
 * NO ANCHOR HELPER, and that is not an omission. The design's TESTIMONIALS
 * element contains no <a> at all, so this file emits no href and owns no
 * data-href-field. Copying onPage() / PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS in here would
 * be an unused function rather than a safeguard — the same call TestimonialsBM
 * made, for the same reason. The section also takes NO id of the page's anchor
 * set (menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main): the design gives this
 * section none of them. The single id it does emit exists only to hang
 * aria-labelledby off the headline.
 *
 * CONTRAST — COMPUTED here (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG 2.x) from the hexes
 * the wrapper declares, not copied and not estimated. The method was checked
 * first by reproducing two of the wrapper's own published figures exactly
 * (accent/paper 5.63 and ink/card 16.86):
 *   quote --ink on --card 16.86 · name --muted on --card 6.01 ·
 *   stars --accent on --card 5.96 · eyebrow --accent-deep on --lilac 7.40 ·
 *   badge --accent-deep on --lilac 7.40 · --plum on --pink-2 8.73 ·
 *   --on-sage on --sage 6.40. Nothing here is below AA.
 *   TWO CAVEATS rather than a clean bill: --muted is remapped by the engine to
 *   palette.inkSoft, and --accent-deep is aliased to palette.primaryDeep, so
 *   those two figures are the NATIVE values and not a guarantee under every
 *   scheme pick. --lilac, --pink, --pink-2, --sage, --on-sage and --plum were
 *   each grepped against genericThemeOverrides.ts and are absent from it, so
 *   every ground in this file holds still while its label may move — which is
 *   the wrapper's own .chip exposure, not something introduced here.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE. The wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 type pass and this file
 * never touches .h2's font-size, so the headline ladder stays the wrapper's and
 * cannot be inverted from here. THERE IS NO MULTI-COLUMN GRID TO COLLAPSE AT
 * 900px: the rail is one row at every width by design — that IS the phone
 * pattern, and it is what the design draws. Instead of stepping down a column
 * count, the card narrows continuously through max-width:86vw, so a 360px
 * viewport gets a card that fits inside the rail with the next one peeking, and
 * every scoped type size in this file only ever DECREASES as the viewport
 * narrows (quote 16.5→15.5, stars 14→13, name 13→12.5, note 13→12.5, badge
 * 38→34px). Nothing pushes the page sideways: the rail scrolls itself, the body
 * clips the axis, and every text node carries overflow-wrap:anywhere. There is
 * no button in this section, so the white-space/overflow-wrap pair a full-width
 * label needs does not arise.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const test = content.testimonials || {};

/* Coerce at EVERY read — see COERCED AT EVERY READ above. A string passes; a
   finite number passes as its digits; null, undefined, NaN, an array and an
   object are not content and become ''. Nothing downstream calls a string
   method on raw input, so no shape from the pipeline can fail the build. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const tag = str(test.tag).trim();
const headline = str(test.headline).trim();
const note = str(test.source).trim();

type Item = {
  quote?: unknown;
  who?: unknown;
  author?: unknown;
  name?: unknown;
  rating?: unknown;
};

/* Tolerate both shapes at every read: the canonical object with .items, and the
   legacy bare array parked at content.testimonials. */
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(test.items)
  ? test.items
  : (Array.isArray(test) ? (test as Item[]) : []);

/* A rating is a CLAIM about a customer. Only a finite number becomes stars; it
   is rounded, capped at 5, and anything that rounds below 1 yields 0 — which
   draws the soft bar, not a star. Never widen this.
   READ THROUGH str(), the same gate every other field here uses: a bare
   Number() is wider than this file's own rule, because Number(true) is 1 and
   Number(['5']) is 5 — so a boolean or a single-element array arriving from the
   extractor would have printed a star nobody typed. */
const starsOf = (it: Item): number => {
  const r = Number(str(it.rating).trim());
  if (!Number.isFinite(r)) return 0;
  const n = Math.round(r);
  return n >= 1 ? Math.min(5, n) : 0;
};

const STAR = '★';

/* The three tinted badge pairs this palette actually names. The design cycles
   four; its fourth is the untokenised warm amber described above. */
const BADGE_TINTS = ['a', 'b', 'c'];

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index, then drop the rows that
   cannot render an honest card. Gating on `rows` rather than `items` also means
   a block of blank rows hides instead of shipping four empty white cards. */
const rows = items
  .map((raw, i: number) => {
    /* THE ROW IS COERCED BEFORE ANY FIELD IS READ. A null row would throw on
       `.quote` and fail the build; a bare string row is the quote the owner
       typed and is kept, not dropped. */
    const it: Item = raw && typeof raw === 'object' ? (raw as Item) : ({ quote: raw } as Item);
    /* normalizeBlock aliases name/author onto `who` only when `who` is null, so
       a row carrying who:'' keeps its blank; this chain is `||` and not `??` so
       that row still prints the name the owner did type. */
    const who = str(it.who).trim() || str(it.author).trim() || str(it.name).trim();
    return {
      /* the quote keeps its own leading/trailing space — only the GUARD is
         trimmed, so a blank-looking quote cannot open an empty card */
      quote: str(it.quote),
      who,
      /* Array.from, not [0]: charAt would split an emoji or an astral-plane
         letter in half and print a replacement glyph. */
      initial: Array.from(who)[0] || '',
      stars: starsOf(it),
      i,
    };
  })
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.quote.trim()));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline || note);
/* The rail's accessible name is the owner's headline or nothing at all. */
const HEAD_ID = 'sip-reviews-h';
const railNameAttrs = headline ? { role: 'group', 'aria-labelledby': HEAD_ID } : {};
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sip-reviews">
    {hasHead && (
      <div class="wrap sip-head reveal">
        {tag && <span class="kick sip-kick" data-field="testimonials.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2" id={HEAD_ID} data-field="testimonials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        {note && <p class="sip-note" data-field="testimonials.source">{note}</p>}
      </div>
    )}
    <div class="sip-rail" tabindex="0" {...railNameAttrs}>
      {rows.map((row, k) => (
        <figure class="sip-card reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
          {row.stars > 0 ? (
            /* Drawn from the owner's own number, and named with that same
               number so the glyphs are never decoration-only. */
            <span class="sip-stars" role="img" aria-label={`Rated ${row.stars} out of 5`}>
              <span aria-hidden="true">{STAR.repeat(row.stars)}</span>
            </span>
          ) : (
            /* Where the design hardcodes five stars. A soft pink bar on the same
               beat: same visual opening, same card height, no claim. */
            <span class="sip-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          )}
          <blockquote data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.quote`} set:html={row.quote}></blockquote>
          {row.who && (
            <figcaption class="sip-by">
              {row.initial && (
                <span class={`sip-badge sip-badge--${BADGE_TINTS[k % BADGE_TINTS.length]}`} aria-hidden="true">{row.initial}</span>
              )}
              <span class="sip-who" data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.who`}>{row.who}</span>
            </figcaption>
          )}
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* The design's own clip. The rail carries its own overflow-x, so this is the
     belt to that pair of braces: it keeps a reveal transform on a full-bleed
     row from ever registering against the page. */
  .sip-reviews { overflow: hidden; }

  /* head · the shared .wrap gutter, centred, 30px of air under it (design) */
  .sip-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 30px; }

  /* The eyebrow keeps the .kick PRIMITIVE and adds only the design's pill: the
     lilac ground, the deep-purple label, the 100px radius. Font size is NOT
     restated, so the wrapper's own 520px step on .kick still lands and this
     section cannot invert the eyebrow ladder. Letter-spacing is the design's
     .14em rather than the primitive's .18em. */
  .sip-kick {
    background-color: var(--lilac);
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    max-width: 100%;
    text-align: center;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The wrapper's .h2 is left-aligned with a BOTTOM margin; the design's head is
     centred with the margin on TOP. The 22ch cap from the wrapper is kept and
     centred by the auto margins so a long owner headline cannot run the full
     width. NO font-size is touched at ANY breakpoint in this file, so the
     clamp inversion an audit found on an earlier template — a 900-query floor
     sitting above a 520-query cap — cannot be reintroduced here. */
  .sip-reviews .h2 {
    margin: 0.24em auto 6px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* the small muted line under the headline (design: 13px / 600) */
  .sip-note {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* rail · FULL BLEED, not inside .wrap: the design runs the row edge to edge
     so the next card peeks past the gutter and reads as swipeable. The inline
     padding is the .wrap primitive's own auto margin, recomputed: .wrap is
     min(--maxw, 92vw) centred, so HALF OF WHAT IT LEAVES OVER *is* its gutter,
     and card 1's left edge lands on the headline's at every width. It is
     measured in % of this row — whose containing block is the section content
     box, the very box .wrap centres inside — and not in vw, because a vw
     counts the scrollbar that box does not, which drifted the card half a
     scrollbar right of the headline on every desktop. max(0px, …) only stops a
     sub-200px viewport computing a negative padding, which would drop the
     declaration and lose the gutter altogether. */
  .sip-rail {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 0 max(0px, (100% - min(var(--maxw), 92vw)) / 2) 20px;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
  }

  /* card · the design's soft white tile. The flex column with a 14px gap is the
     design's own spacing mechanism, and it is what lets the attribution be
     pushed to the bottom edge with margin-top:auto so a short quote and a long
     one still line their names up across the row. max-width is the whole
     narrow-viewport story: the fixed 360px holds until 86vw is the smaller of
     the two, after which the card shrinks continuously instead of stepping. */
  .sip-card {
    margin: 0;
    flex: none;
    width: 360px;
    max-width: 86vw;
    min-width: 0;
    scroll-snap-align: center;
    background-color: var(--card);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: 28px 26px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  /* the rating mark. Both variants occupy the same ~24px beat so a card WITH a
     rating and a card without stay on the same baseline in the same row.
     line-height is DECLARED rather than inherited so that height is
     deterministic — and 1.72 is precisely the value it WOULD inherit from the
     wrapper's body, so this declaration changes nothing on screen. */
  .sip-stars {
    display: block;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.72;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    color: var(--accent);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The bar that replaces the design's hardcoded star row: --pink used as a
     GROUND, which is the only way this palette allows it. 3px on a pill radius
     because nothing in this design is sharp; 10.5px margins reproduce the 24px
     the star row occupies. */
  .sip-bar {
    display: block;
    width: 64px;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 3px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    background-color: var(--pink);
    margin: 10.5px 0;
  }

  /* quote · the UA gives <blockquote> a 40px side margin, so the reset here is
     load-bearing, not tidying. */
  .sip-card blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's emphasis inside a
     quote is only reachable through :global(). It keeps the quote's own ink:
     the purple colour change is the HEADLINE signature, and repainting a
     customer's words purple would read as the tea bar talking, not the
     customer. Nunito Sans has a real italic, so the italic here is genuine and
     not the synthesised oblique Baloo 2 would give. */
  .sip-card blockquote :global(em),
  .sip-card blockquote :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: inherit; }

  /* attribution · pinned to the card floor (design) */
  .sip-by {
    margin-top: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 11px;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* the derived initial. Uppercased in CSS, never in the string. */
  .sip-badge {
    flex: none;
    width: 38px;
    height: 38px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
